course: dictionary
lesson: 6394
title: Health words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words come from health and science, and you can work them out from the words around them. «The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella in two doses in early childhood.» Mumps sits in a list with measles and rubella, and a vaccine protects against them, so mumps must be an illness too.
explain: Look at the phrases that go with a word. People «have mumps» and «catch mumps», and the word catch tells you it can pass from one person to another. Mumps is a noun for an illness, and it is used with a singular verb: you say «Mumps is painful», not «Mumps are painful».
explain: When you meet a health word you do not know, ask yourself what the sentence is about. Is it about a cure, a cause, or how an illness spreads? Then swap your meaning into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
fact: Mumps is an infectious illness, so it can spread from person to person.
fact: Mumps is caused by a virus, which is a tiny germ too small to see.
fact: Mumps makes the glands under the ears swell, so the cheeks and jaw puff up and hurt.
fact: Mumps spreads through coughs, sneezes and saliva.
fact: The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella, and it is offered to children in many countries, including the UK, USA and Australia.
exercise: What does mumps mean? | *An infectious illness that makes the glands under the ears swell | A cough medicine that you drink from a spoon | A sore throat caused by cold air | A kind of vaccine given in a jab
exercise: «The MMR vaccine protects against measles, mumps and rubella.» Mumps is grouped with which two words? | *Measles and rubella | Cheeks and jaw | Vaccine and jab | Coughs and sneezes
exercise: Which sentence uses mumps correctly? | Mumps are painful and make the glands swell | Mumps were painful and made the glands swell at once, every day | *Mumps is painful and makes the glands swell | Mumps am painful and makes the glands swell
test: How does mumps spread from person to person? | Through cold water and wet feet | Through eating green vegetables | Through sunlight on the skin | *Through coughs, sneezes and saliva
test: What does the MMR vaccine protect against? | Colds and flu only | Broken bones and bruises | *Measles, mumps and rubella
exam: What is the MMR jab for? | *Protecting children against measles, mumps and rubella | Stopping children from growing taller | Making the glands under the ears swell | Curing a cough quickly at home
